  "scenario": "A person types for about six hours a day and is trying to understand why one mechanical keyboard can cost about 40 dollars while premium boards are often discussed around 150 to 200 dollars, sometimes with similar broad switch labels. They want to know which paid-for differences can affect daily typing, durability, serviceability, or fit, which differences mainly change sound or hobby experience, and how to set a defensible purchase boundary. The available material contains a current catalog ladder from 39.99 to 149.99 dollars, community reports about switch feel, sound, build compromises, modifications, and street pricing, plus concept pages about layout, posture, construction, and durability. It does not contain a controlled head-to-head trial or a complete 150 to 200 dollar enthusiast catalog sample.",
  "constraints": [
    "treat_catalog_prices_as_snapshot_observations_not_stable_market_values",
    "do_not_treat_a_shared_color_or_switch_family_label_as_a_complete_board_equivalence",
    "separate_layout_connectivity_hot_swap_keycaps_case_actuation_display_and_accessory_features",
    "separate_daily_typing_fit_quietness_tactile_feel_durability_and_serviceability_from_close_up_sound_preferences",
    "treat_forum_posts_and_comments_as_scoped_experiences_not_population_prevalence_or_controlled_tests",
    "do_not_infer_that_a_higher_price_guarantees_better_typing_sound_or_lifetime",
    "do_not_claim_that_the_material_establishes_a_universal_150_to_200_dollar_cutoff",
    "end_with_an_operational_purchase_boundary_and_a_short_in_person_or_return_window_test"
  ],
  "candidate_actions": [
    "compare_the_observed_price_and_feature_ladder",
    "separate_switch_labels_from_the_rest_of_the_keyboard_system",
