  ],
  "rules": {
    "observed_price_feature_ladder_v1": "The frozen catalog pages show five snapshots. A 39.99 dollar 81-key listing advertises wireless and wired modes, hot-swap sockets, RGB, and generic blue switches. A 55.50 dollar Keychron C2 listing advertises a full-size wired layout, hot-swap sockets, Gateron G Pro Blue switches, and ABS frame and keycaps. An 84.99 dollar Keychron K10 listing advertises a full-size wireless and wired layout, Gateron Brown switches, and a large battery. A 149.99 dollar Durgod Hades listing advertises a compact 68-key layout, an aluminium chassis, PBT keycaps, NKRO, software, and Gateron Silent Brown among its switch options. A different 149.99 dollar Apex Pro listing advertises adjustable actuation, an OLED display, an aluminum-alloy frame, a wrist rest, RGB, and other gaming controls. A community post reports one Matias board at a 70 dollar new-in-box street price while seller prices were 160 and 170 dollars, illustrating price-channel variation but not a stable market tier. The ladder demonstrates that spend may go to different bundles rather than one quality axis. It does not demonstrate a controlled typing, acoustic, or longevity ordering, and its highest catalog price is 149.99 dollars.",
    "switch_board_and_sound_scope_v1": "A broad blue or brown switch label does not specify the case, plate, keycaps, stabilizers, layout, mounting, damping, firmware, connectivity, assembly, or quality control. Community participants in the frozen material explicitly argue that board, plate, keycaps, and chassis shape sound, while another thread reports that the cheapest tested silent switches were quietest but a different switch had the best tactile feel. This separates acoustic level from tactile preference and shows why a close-up sound clip cannot settle a six-hour typing decision. These are scoped experiences and comments, not controlled acoustic measurements.",
    "daily_use_and_build_tradeoffs_v1": "The catalog shows that a 55.50 dollar full-size board can advertise Gateron G Pro Blue switches and ABS construction while a 149.99 dollar compact board can advertise an aluminium chassis, PBT keycaps, and a Gateron Silent Brown option; these listing differences do not establish the resulting sustained feel or sound. The Gamakay review reports a budget silent switch used as a daily driver in an open office. The reviewer liked its quietness and typing result but described cheaper-feeling plastic housing, thin and flimsy pins, and installation difficulty. The NiZ thread lists switches, lubrication, stabilizers, plates, foam, and tape modifications before reporting that one out-of-box board sounded as good to that author as a fully modified board and felt good to type on. The Matias thread reports a new-in-box street price of 70 dollars versus seller prices of 160 and 170 dollars, quiet office use, disagreement about quiet-switch feel, and comments about chatter and heavy daily-use durability. These pages show criterion-specific tradeoffs, modification time, quality-control risk, and price-channel variation, but they cannot establish universal failure rates or value rankings.",
    "layout_posture_and_construction_v1": "The computer-keyboard source distinguishes full-size and tenkeyless layouts and notes that the number pad is commonly omitted for compactness or preference. The ergonomic-keyboard source says a relaxed posture keeps forearms parallel to the ground and wrists straight, describes compact layouts as freeing mouse space and reducing outward arm rotation, and explains how positive slope can require wrist extension. The Model M source links one specific design's heavy steel backplate and strong plastic frame with decades of surviving units and describes a distinct feel and sound. These concept examples support checking layout, height, slope, mouse reach, construction, and repairability separately. They do not prove that every heavy or expensive keyboard is durable or ergonomic for every person.",
    "person_specific_purchase_boundary_v1": "For a six-hour typist, first define non-negotiables: needed keys and layout, desk and mouse reach, connection mode, noise limits, switch force and feel, keyboard height and slope, hot-swap or repair needs, and acceptable modification time. Compare complete boards against those requirements, then test a switch sample or the actual board for sustained typing, stabilized keys, off-center presses, noise in the real room, firmware and connection behavior, and wrist posture. Use a return window when possible. Spend beyond the least expensive passing option only for a named, verified benefit. Because the catalog stops at 149.99 dollars, the only 160 and 170 dollar figures are a community price report, and there is no controlled cross-tier trial, the material cannot identify one universal price cutoff or one objectively best board at that cutoff.",
    "evidence_bounded_keyboard_value_decision_v1": "The admissible conclusion is to report the observed feature allocation without converting price into a quality score, separate switch label from whole-board construction, prioritize the buyer's daily typing and fit criteria over close-up sound, and choose the least expensive board that passes an exact hands-on checklist. If no tested option passes or exact model evidence is missing, state that no board or universal cutoff can be selected from this material."
  },
